# Dedicated SELinux domain for the Tarish app (dev.tarish.app).
#
# WHY (security review, finding #28). The app used to run as `platform_app` — a class every
# platform-signed app with its own uid lands in — so ANY such app could drive ITarishService:
# enumerate peers, send files, and setPolicy to switch on auto-accept. Its own domain scopes
# the service's `find`/`call` to ONLY this app; tarishsharingd.te no longer grants platform_app
# anything, which is what actually closes the hole.
#
# ENFORCING. The rule set below was derived, not guessed: the domain shipped `permissive` for
# one build (2026092313) and every rule here comes from a denial actually logged on hardware
# while the app was driven through launch, all four screens, the settings toggles, the
# document picker, BLE scan/advertise, discovery, and a send.
#
# Permissive collection can only ever be half the answer -- `dontaudit` suppresses denials in
# permissive mode too, so a silent log does not prove a complete policy (BUILD-NOTES 28). The
# other half is this: run enforcing and exercise the app for real. A missing rule then shows
# up as a feature that does not work, which is the test that actually matters.
#
# coredomain: the app ships on a core partition (system_ext/priv-app), and Treble requires
# every platform app's domain to be a coredomain type.
type tarish_app, domain, coredomain;
app_domain(tarish_app)

# The whole point of the domain: reach the Tarish daemon's binder service, both ways
# (the daemon calls back through ITarishCallback).
allow tarish_app tarish_service:service_manager find;
binder_call(tarish_app, tarishsharingd)
binder_call(tarishsharingd, tarish_app)

# --- derived from AVCs on the permissive build (2026-09-23, cycle 1) ---
# The standard app-accessible framework services (activity, activity_task, wifi,
# bluetooth_manager, autofill, uimode, netstats, gpu, textservices, voiceinteraction,
# content_capture, ...). Each app domain must be granted this EXPLICITLY — it is not inherited
# via appdomain (priv_app_all, gmscore_app, mediaprovider_app all grant it the same way).
allow tarish_app app_api_service:service_manager find;

# The GPU RenderThread reads /sys/.../uevent while rendering the UI.
allow tarish_app sysfs:file r_file_perms;

# --- derived from AVCs (cycle 2: a real AirDrop receive from an iPhone) ---
# Read a received file through the fd the daemon hands us:
#
#   avc: denied { read } for comm="tarishsharingd" path="/data/misc/tarish/inbox/IMG_0017.MOV"
#        scontext=u:r:tarish_app tcontext=u:object_r:tarish_data_file tclass=file
#
# Note whose names those are: `comm` is the DAEMON, `scontext` is the APP. That is binder's
# security_binder_transfer_file() -- when a service passes a file descriptor, the kernel checks
# the RECEIVING domain against the file's type, and logs it against the sending process. So
# this is not the app opening anything; it is the app being handed an fd the daemon already
# opened, and the check is whether it may read what arrived.
#
# `read` alone, deliberately, and it is worth seeing why that is tight rather than lax:
#
#   - no `open`, and no `dir search` on tarish_data_file, so the app CANNOT open the inbox or
#     walk it. It can only read fds the daemon chose to give it, which keeps the daemon the
#     gatekeeper of which received file the app ever sees.
#   - no `write`: the app copies the bytes out to MediaStore and never writes back. The fd is
#     opened O_RDONLY, and file_to_av() maps that to FILE__READ only, so `write` would be an
#     unused grant.
#   - `fd use` is already covered by binder_call(tarish_app, tarishsharingd) above.
#
# Without this the receive still completes -- the daemon writes the file -- but the app's read
# of the fd fails, so nothing is copied to MediaStore and the file never reaches the gallery.
allow tarish_app tarish_data_file:file read;
